.TH CHTTPSVR_ENGINE_STOP 3 "2026-07-14" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_engine_stop \- signal the engine to stop
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

void chttpsvr_engine_stop(void);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Signals the shared reactor of chttpserver, and the idle-timeout sweep
thread beside it, to stop.  It forces them down even while other
chttpsvr instances in the same process still hold a reference and are
still fully started.  This reactor is separate from the engine of
chttpclient.  A call to this function therefore has no effect on the
async work of chttpclient in the same process, that is, on
chttpclient_do_async, chttpclient_do_async_streaming,
chttpclient_do_pooled and chttpclient_do_pooled_streaming.  It returns
at once.  Use chttpsvr_engine_wait() to block until the stop
completes.

The library quiesces every chttpsvr instance that is still registered
in the process before it tears the shared reactor down.  It does this
whether or not the instance is still started.  It quiesces each one in
exactly the way that chttpsvr_destroy() does: it closes the listener,
drains the in-flight requests, closes the idle and parked connections
and the requests that wait for body memory, answers the queued
streaming requests with 503, shuts the worker pools down, and frees
the engine reference of that instance.
This is what makes the ONE chttpsvr_destroy(srv) call in the
signal-handler shutdown pattern below safe.  That call is safe even
though it runs after the library already tore the engine down.  The
library already quiesced srv above, so that call only frees the memory
of the server that remains.  It repeats no teardown work.  This does
not make repeated destroy calls on the same handle safe.  A second
chttpsvr_destroy(srv) call on a handle that an earlier call already
destroyed stays a fatal error, with abort() and SIGABRT.  This is true
for this path and for every other one.

    static void on_signal(int sig) { (void)sig; chttpsvr_engine_stop(); }
    /* ... */
    if (chttpsvr_start(srv, &cfg) != ccol_success) {
        chttpsvr_destroy(srv);
        chttpsvr_engine_wait();   /* the stop can be incomplete */
        return 1;
    }
    chttpsvr_engine_wait();   /* returns once on_signal fires */
    chttpsvr_destroy(srv);    /* safe: srv was already quiesced above */

This function does not block, and it is async-signal-safe.  It is safe
to call it directly from a POSIX signal handler, for example for
SIGINT or SIGTERM.  It is the only function in this module that is
documented as async-signal-safe.  chttpsvr_stop() is not.  See the
WARNINGS section of that function.  Do not call chttpsvr_stop()
directly from a signal handler.

The library installs no signal handlers.  An application must connect
this function to whatever signal or shutdown mechanism it uses.

It is safe to call this function before the reactor ever starts, where
it does nothing.

You can call it more than once.  You can also call it while the drain
of an earlier call still runs.  Two SIGTERMs that arrive moments apart
do this, and so does an extra defensive call from the shutdown code of
an application.  A repeated or overlapping call does nothing.  It is
never a second teardown.
.fi
